Q: Nuxt Fonts does not generate the correct `@font-face` when the font is set as the default font in TailwindCSS v4 A: <p>It's worth mentioning that the <code>@nuxt/fonts</code> package does not analyze source files directly, but rather the output produced by various processors (such as TailwindCSS), which may differ from the original source.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If you previously set <code>processCSSVariables</code> to <code>true</code> for Tailwind v4 support, <strong>it is no longer needed</strong> or recommended for v0.11.0 and above. (<a href="https://fonts.nuxt.com/get-started/usage#v4" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Source</a>)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Interestingly, contrary to the test results, it seems that font names are only detected correctly when they are placed directly in a <code>font-family</code> property or when the variable name starts with <code>--font-</code>.</p>
<p>But it's worth noting that all the mentioned tests use the now <code>processCSSVariables</code> property:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://github.com/nuxt/fonts/blob/d551db7de7985f2e7849508fc0db52ff6eef820d/test/parse.test.ts#L404" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>nuxt/fonts</code> - parse.test.ts line 404</a></li>
</ul>
<h3>Solution</h3>
<p>It seems that enabling again the <code>processCSSVariables</code> property will allow automatic detection of any variable. So far, the <code>--font-</code> prefixed variables are recognized only because of TailwindCSS v4, as TailwindCSS v4 requests all fonts to be added under the <code>--font-*</code> namespace.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export default defineNuxtConfig({
  fonts: {
    processCSSVariables: true,
  }
})
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import 'tailwindcss';

@theme {
  --default-font-family: &quot;Rubik Storm&quot;;
  --font-custom: &quot;Comic Sans MS&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Alternative #1 (without <code>processCSSVariables</code>)</h3>
<p>A possible solution, therefore, is to make the font a static part of the source:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import 'tailwindcss';

@theme {
  --default-font-family: &quot;Rubik Storm&quot;;
  --font-custom: &quot;Comic Sans MS&quot;;
}

.never-used-but-triggers-a-download {
  font-family: &quot;Rubik Storm&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Alternative #2 (without <code>processCSSVariables</code>)</h3>
<p>A possible solution is to declare the font you want to use in <code>nuxt.config.ts</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export default defineNuxtConfig({
  fonts: {
    families: [
      { name: 'Rubik Storm', provider: 'google', global: true },
    ]
  }
})
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import 'tailwindcss';

@theme {
  --default-font-family: &quot;Rubik Storm&quot;;
  --font-custom: &quot;Comic Sans MS&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>In this case, you don't rely on autodetection, but explicitly force the inclusion of the &quot;Rubik Storm&quot;.</p>
<h3>Alternative #3 (without <code>processCSSVariables</code>)</h3>
<p>There is an alternative approach: TailwindCSS can inline values in place of variables using <code>@theme inline</code>. This completely bypasses <code>--default-font-family</code>, and the font-family name will appear directly as a <code>font-family: ...</code> value somewhere in the TailwindCSS-generated output. As a result, Nuxt Fonts will be able to detect it.</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import 'tailwindcss';

@theme inline {
  --default-font-family: &quot;Rubik Storm&quot;;
}

@theme {
  --font-custom: &quot;Comic Sans MS&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Alternatively, with this method you can replace <code>--default-font-family</code> with another variable, e.g., <code>--font-rubik</code>, and at the same time declare the <code>font-*</code> utility namespace under the name <code>rubik</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import 'tailwindcss';

@theme {
  --font-rubik: &quot;Rubik Storm&quot;;
  --font-custom: &quot;Comic Sans MS&quot;;
}

@theme inline {
  --default-font-family: var(--font-rubik);
}
</code></pre>
<p>In this case, the <code>--default-font-family</code> references in the output are replaced with <code>--font-rubik</code>. Since <code>--font-rubik</code> starts with <code>--font-</code>, Nuxt Fonts will detect it and add the corresponding <code>@font-face</code>.</p>

Q: reactor-netty http server authentication A: <h1>Pure Reactor Netty Solution:</h1>
<p>There are undoubtably many ways one could implement &quot;BeforeFilter&quot; functionality with Reactor Netty.</p>
<p>The class RectorNettyFiltersDemo below build's on Gerd's answer, and models the Filters as <code>Mono&lt;Boolean&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>The method <code>withAuthFilter()</code> demonstrates chaining of the service to the filter using a flatMap().</p>
<p>i.e. If the &quot;filter&quot; <code>authenticateRequest()</code> is successful, the ServiceHandler is called to process the request itself.
If <code>authenticateRequest()</code> does not authenticate the request, then <code>notAuthenticated()</code> is called instead, returning a 401.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>  private static RequestHandler withAuthFilter(ServiceHandler handler) {
    return (req, res) -&gt;
      authenticateRequest(req) // &quot;hard-coded&quot; filter
        .flatMap(ok -&gt; ok
            ? handler.apply(req, res) // chain the service if filter ok
            : notAuthenticated(req, res)
    );
  }
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Getting more advanced ...</strong></p>
<p>A given route may require more than one BeforeFilter.</p>
<p>The method <code>reducedFilters()</code> uses a <code>Flux</code> to merge a List of <code>Mono&lt;Boolean&gt;</code>s (the filters), and then <code>flux.reduce()</code> to reduce the filters to one.</p>
<p>The method <code>failFastFilters()</code> evaluates each filter one by one in a loop, allowing a fail-fast in any filter returns false.
A small hack is that we use an <code>AtomicBoolean</code> to get the result from the lambda of the (non-blocking) <code>filter.subscribe()</code>.</p>
<p><strong>The code:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>package org.acme;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean;
import java.util.function.BiFunction;

import io.netty.handler.codec.http.HttpResponseStatus;
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import reactor.netty.DisposableServer;
import reactor.netty.http.server.HttpServer;
import reactor.netty.http.server.HttpServerRequest;
import reactor.netty.http.server.HttpServerResponse;
import reactor.netty.http.server.HttpServerRoutes;

public class RectorNettyFiltersDemo {

  public static void main(String[] args) {

    DisposableServer server =
        HttpServer.create()
                  .port(8080)
                  .route(routes -&gt; buildRoutes(routes))
                  .bindNow();

    server.onDispose()
          .block();

  }

  private static HttpServerRoutes buildRoutes(HttpServerRoutes routes) {

    // call the service processRequest() directly, without any route filters
    routes.get(&quot;/public&quot;,        
        (req, res) -&gt;  res.sendString(processRequest(req, res)));

    // All the routes below use some form of &quot;BeforeFilter&quot; 
    // called before the main service.

    // This uses one hardcoded Authentication filter
    routes.get(&quot;/secure1&quot;, withAuthFilter((req, res) -&gt;
      res.sendString(processRequest(req, res)).then()));


    // This version allows the filter and the service handler to be configured
    routes.get(&quot;/secure2&quot;, withFilters(
        (req, res) -&gt; authenticateRequest(req),
        (req, res) -&gt; res.sendString(processRequest(req, res)).then()));

    // This evaluates all filters first i.e. no fast fail!  
    // multiple filters will be reduced to one by reducedFilters()
    routes.get(&quot;/secure3&quot;, withFilters(
        (req, res) -&gt; reducedFilters(getFilters(req)),
        (req, res) -&gt; res.sendString(processRequest(req, res)).then()
    ));

    // Here we use Mono.defer() to ensure the filters are only evaluated if called.
    routes.get(&quot;/secure4&quot;, withFilters(
        (req, res) -&gt; failFastFilters(getDeferredFilters(req)),
        (req, res) -&gt; res.sendString(processRequest(req, res)).then()
    ));

    return routes;

  }

  private static List&lt;Mono&lt;Boolean&gt;&gt; getFilters(HttpServerRequest req) {  
    List&lt;Mono&lt;Boolean&gt;&gt; filters = Arrays.asList(authenticateRequest(req),
                                                anotherFilter(req),
                                                yetAnotherFilter(req));
    return filters;
  }

  private static List&lt;Mono&lt;Boolean&gt;&gt; getDeferredFilters(HttpServerRequest req) {
    return Arrays.asList(Mono.defer(() -&gt; (authenticateRequest(req))),
                         Mono.defer(() -&gt; (anotherFilter(req))),
                         Mono.defer(() -&gt; (yetAnotherFilter(req))));
  }

  private static RequestHandler withAuthFilter(ServiceHandler handler) {
    return (req, res) -&gt;
      authenticateRequest(req) // &quot;hard-coded&quot; filter
        .flatMap(ok -&gt; ok
          ? handler.apply(req, res) // chain the service if filter ok
          : notAuthenticated(req, res));
  }

  private static RequestHandler withFilters(FilterHandler filterHandler, ServiceHandler serviceHandler) {  
    return (req, res) -&gt;
      filterHandler.apply(req, res) // filter handler from parameter
        .flatMap(ok -&gt; ok
          ? serviceHandler.apply(req, res)
          : notAuthenticated(req, res));  
  }

  private static Mono&lt;Boolean&gt; reducedFilters(List&lt;Mono&lt;Boolean&gt;&gt; filters) {
    // https://stackoverflow.com/questions/73857084/reducing-a-list-of-monoboolean  
    Flux&lt;Boolean&gt; flux = Flux.merge(filters);
    return flux.reduce((a, b) -&gt; a &amp;&amp; b);
  }

  private static Mono&lt;Boolean&gt; failFastFilters(List&lt;Mono&lt;Boolean&gt;&gt; filters) {
    for (Mono&lt;Boolean&gt; filter : filters) {
      // https://stackoverflow.com/questions/30026824/modifying-local-variable-from-inside-lambda  
      AtomicBoolean evaluated = new AtomicBoolean(true);  
      filter.subscribe(result -&gt; evaluated.set(result));
      if (!evaluated.get()) {
        return Mono.just(false); // fail-fast if any filter returns false, don't check any other filters
      }  
    }
    return Mono.just(true); // if we got here, all filters returned true 8-)
  }

  // The filters: set to true or false as desired for testing.
  private static Mono&lt;Boolean&gt; authenticateRequest(HttpServerRequest req) {
    // here is where we call the authentication logic.
    //Hard-coded to true / false for this simple demo.  
    return Mono.just(true).name(&quot;authenticateRequest&quot;);
  }

  private static Mono&lt;Boolean&gt; anotherFilter(HttpServerRequest req) {
    return Mono.just(false).name(&quot;anotherFilter&quot;);
  }

  private static Mono&lt;Boolean&gt; yetAnotherFilter(HttpServerRequest req) {
    return Mono.just(true).name(&quot;yetAnotherFilter&quot;);
  }

  private static Mono&lt;Void&gt; notAuthenticated(HttpServerRequest req, HttpServerResponse res) {
    return res.status(HttpResponseStatus.UNAUTHORIZED).send().then(); //401
  }

  private static Mono&lt;String&gt; processRequest(HttpServerRequest req, HttpServerResponse  res) {  
    // This is the service, normally much more going on here ....
    return Mono.just(&quot;Hello World! on: &quot;.concat(req.fullPath()));
  }

  // Abstractions to make the core code a little easier to read.
  private static interface RequestHandler extends BiFunction&lt;HttpServerRequest, HttpServerResponse, Mono&lt;Void&gt;&gt; {}
  private static interface ServiceHandler extends BiFunction&lt;HttpServerRequest, HttpServerResponse, Mono&lt;Void&gt;&gt; {}
  private static interface FilterHandler extends BiFunction&lt;HttpServerRequest, HttpServerResponse, Mono&lt;Boolean&gt;&gt; {}

}
</code></pre>
<p>Test call from CURL: <code>curl -v http://localhost:8080/secure4</code></p>
<p>Test call from HttPIE: <code>http -v http://localhost:8080/secure4</code></p>
<h1>and another option....</h1>
<p>While I kept the original question open, I was primarily interested in a pure reactor-netty solution, which implies a modicum of &quot;roll-your-own&quot; code.</p>
<p>But there are many ways to skin a cat: Another option would be to use Spring WebFlux, which wraps Reactor Netty (among other Web Servers), and provides additional functionality out-of-the-box, including filters.</p>
<p>Below is an example implementation of a filter using Spring WebFlux's <code>HandlerFilterFunction </code></p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>package org.acme;

import static org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.RequestPredicates.GET;

import org.springframework.boot.reactor.netty.NettyReactiveWebServerFactory;
import org.springframework.boot.web.server.WebServer;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.server.reactive.HttpHandler;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.HandlerFilterFunction;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.HandlerFunction;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.RouterFunction;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.RouterFunctions;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.ServerRequest;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.ServerResponse;

import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

public class WebApp {

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    WebServer netty = getNetty();
    netty.start();
  }

  private static WebServer getNetty() {
    RouteHandler handler = new RouteHandler();
    RouterFunction&lt;?&gt; routerFunction = routes(handler);
    HttpHandler httpHandler = RouterFunctions.toHttpHandler(routerFunction);
    NettyReactiveWebServerFactory rwsf = new NettyReactiveWebServerFactory();
    return rwsf.getWebServer(httpHandler);
  }

  private static RouterFunction&lt;ServerResponse&gt; routes(RouteHandler routeHandler) {
    RouterFunctions.Builder routesBuilder = RouterFunctions.route();
    routesBuilder.route(GET(&quot;/hello&quot;), routeHandler::handle)
                 .route(GET(&quot;/again&quot;), routeHandler::handle);
    return routesBuilder
      .filter(new AuthenticationFilter())
      .build();   
  }

  private static class RouteHandler {

    public Mono&lt;ServerResponse&gt; handle(final ServerRequest serverRequest) {      
      // assuming we have passed the AuthenticationFilter.filter(),
      // do something useful here to process the request itself.

      Mono&lt;String&gt; body = Mono.just(&quot;Hello you are authenticated on path: &quot;.concat(serverRequest.requestPath().toString()));
      return ServerResponse.ok()
          .body(body, String.class);
    }
  }

  private static class AuthenticationFilter implements HandlerFilterFunction&lt;ServerResponse, ServerResponse&gt; {

    private static boolean authenticateRequest(ServerRequest request) {
      // here is where we call the authentication logic. Hard-coded to false for this simple demo.
      return true;
    }

    @Override
    public Mono&lt;ServerResponse&gt; filter(ServerRequest request, HandlerFunction&lt;ServerResponse&gt; handlerFunction) {
      if (authenticateRequest(request)) {
        return handlerFunction.handle(request); 
      } else {
        return notAuthorized();
      }
    }

    private Mono&lt;ServerResponse&gt;  notAuthorized() {
      return ServerResponse.status(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED).body(Mono.just(&quot;Go away!!!&quot;), String.class); 
    }

  }
}
</code></pre>
<p><em>Note: This is Spring WebFlux without Spring Boot. It may well not be the best way to use WebFlux. We provide it here as an indication that WebFlux offers filtering functionality out-of-the-box that should also be explored.</em></p>

Q: How to get data from database by using an array of ids in codeigniter? A: <p><strong>Controller:-</strong></p>
<pre><code>$toppings = $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('topping');    //Array         
$toppinglist = $this-&gt;toppingmodel-&gt;find_topping($toppings);
</code></pre>
<p><strong>model:-</strong></p>
<pre><code>function find_topping($idArr = array()){         //Array  
 $query = $this-&gt;db-&gt;where_in(&quot;Topping&quot;, $idArr)-&gt;get(&quot;tablename&quot;);
     return $query-&gt;result_array();
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Note:-</strong>
<code>$idArr = array()</code> means</p>
<p>make the function have a default value of array otherwise if something else is passed you'll get an error in your query.</p>
